# 📋 Research Report: The Creation of the C Programming Language

## Executive Summary

The C programming language was created by **Dennis Ritchie** at **Bell Labs** (AT&T) in Murray Hill, New Jersey, with development spanning **1969 to 1973**. The language reached its recognizable form around **1972** and was first used for the Unix kernel rewrite in **1973**. C was designed to enable portability of the Unix operating system across different hardware platforms, evolving from the earlier B and BCPL languages.

---

## Key Findings

### 1. Creator and Institution

The C programming language was created by **Dennis Ritchie** at **Bell Labs** (AT&T) in Murray Hill, New Jersey [1]. Brian Kernighan, co-author of the seminal K&R C book, independently confirms that Ritchie created C at Bell Labs in 1972 [2].

### 2. Timeline of Creation

C was developed over a period spanning **1969 to 1973** [1]. The first significant use of C was for the **Unix kernel rewrite in 1973** [1]. Wikipedia cites the creation period as **1972 to 1973** at Bell Labs [3]. The most authoritative sources converge on a creation date of approximately **1972**, with development continuing through 1973 [1][2][3].

> *Note on timeline discrepancy:* Ritchie's own account (1969–1973) includes early experimentation, while the language named "C" crystallized around 1972. Both are consistent — development began in 1969, and the language as we know it emerged in 1972.

### 3. Predecessors and Motivation

C was preceded by and directly influenced by the **B programming language** (created by Ken Thompson) and **BCPL** (created by Martin Richards) [1]. The primary motivation for creating C was to enable **portability of the Unix operating system** across different hardware platforms, allowing Unix to move beyond the PDP-11 architecture [1].

### 4. Key Milestones After Creation

| Milestone | Year | Description |
|-----------|------|-------------|
| **K&R C** | 1978 | Publication of *"The C Programming Language"* by Kernighan & Ritchie — de facto standard [3] |
| **ANSI C (C89/C90)** | 1989 | First formal standardization by ANSI [3] |
| **C99** | 1999 | Major revision adding inline functions, `long long`, etc. |
| **C11** | 2011 | Updated standard with multi-threading support |
| **C17** | 2018 | Bug-fix revision |
| **C23** | 2024 | Latest revision |

---

